## F4 — the positional extraction accepts any token in the variable slots. Blocking: a regression.
checkPolicy (talk-boundary.test.ts:199) builds the expected argv FROM the actual argv:
  expectedStartArgv(argv[4]) / expectedResumeArgv(argv[2])
So the checks argv[4] === argv[4] and argv[2] === argv[2] always hold. The suite asserts nothing
about those two tokens.
  M18 resume: opts.sessionId -> "--allowedTools=Bash"    exit=0  b1 0 b2 0 b3 0 b4 0   SURVIVES
  M19 start:  file           -> "--allowedTools=Bash"    exit=0  all 0                 SURVIVES
  M21 resume: opts.sessionId -> "--tools=WebFetch"       exit=0  all 0                 SURVIVES
It is a regression. The ROUND-2 test (7566459) kills all three, because its flagsIn set-equality
sees the extra flag-shaped token (script slot-vs-r2.sh):
  M18 exit=1 b1 1 b4 2 | M19 exit=1 b1 1 b4 5 | M21 exit=1 b1 1 b4 2
Vendor behaviour on 2.1.280. slot-probe.mjs ran fully offline: ANTHROPIC_* stripped, base URL set
to 127.0.0.1:9, no request left the machine. The sandbox also denied the CLI's datadog telemetry.
  start baseline         init: permissionMode=dontAsk tools=[Bash,Glob,Grep,Read]
  M19 start slot         exit 1 "Append system prompt file not found: …/--allowedTools=Bash"
  M18 / M21 resume slot  exit 1 "--resume requires a valid session ID or session title when used with --print"
So none of the three widens anything today. All of them fail closed.
- On start, `--append-system-prompt-file <file>` has a REQUIRED value, so the vendor always
  takes the token as a filename.
- On resume, `-r, --resume [value]` has an OPTIONAL value. A flag-shaped token is parsed as a
  flag. Today the only thing stopping M18 is print mode's "requires a session ID" error.
- If the vendor ever lets `-p --resume` fall back to the latest session, M18 becomes an unrestricted
  Bash grant, and this suite would stay green.
Why it blocks although the vendor fails closed: my round-2 advisories M11–M14 were pre-existing
gaps. This one is coverage the branch HAD at 7566459 and lost in this round's diff, in a
permission-boundary test.
Class census: I read every positional or value read in the file. Those are argv[ on lines 69/70
(valuesAfterAll, exact-name match), 199, 237/265 (valuesAfterAll --allowedTools), and the
.includes("--resume") calls on lines 288/289/303, which only pick the form. Only line 199 builds
an expected token from the actual argv. The class has exactly two instances, argv[4] (start) and
argv[2] (resume), both on line 199.
Remedy: stop extracting what the test already knows, and constrain what it can't know. It is a
few lines in the owned test file:
  - b1 resume: the test passes sessionId "sess-fixture", so expect it literally. The header
    comment's "neither side can predict in advance" is false for b1.
  - Every remaining extracted token (b4 talk/tick resume ids, every start file): require that it
    does not start with "-". Also require that the start file ends with "/system-prompt.md". The
    file's own helper comment already states "none of this profile's flag values … do" start with "-".
  Pass condition: M18, M19 and M21 exit=1 on the new test, and M10–M15/M20 still exit=1.

