## Judgment 3: attended live smoke — the security half is accepted; the residue does not block
Evidence I read:
  - /tmp/claude-1000/w044-smoke-part1.txt (the reply, verbatim below).
  - The smoke studio's own talk records: timeline/builder-1.jsonl, receipts/builder-1/219b24dc-….json
    and sessions/builder-1.json.
  - The checkout the smoke ran from, /tmp/claude-1000/w044smoke @ 7566459.
    `git diff --quiet 45ee35a -- packages/shim packages/cli/src/talk.ts` is empty, so the deployed
    policy IS the 45ee35a policy.
  - Its shim resolves to its own tree (/tmp/claude-1000/w044smoke/packages/shim).
  - opera/W-002.md in the smoke studio is byte-identical to examples/sample-studio@45ee35a.
What it proves:
  - greenlight, plain: DENIED pre-execution with the vendor's dontAsk message ("Permission to use
    Bash has been denied because Claude Code is running in don't ask mode").
  - `check … && greenlight …`: DENIED as a whole. Vendor-side compound matching is the one thing
    the stub-based suite cannot prove (brief, "Vendor-side matching … The smoke checks it once").
    It is now checked once on the installed version.
  - Standalone `check` and the bare `bisellium`: ADMITTED. The Bash tool executed, and the 127
    came from the shell, not from the permission layer. So the allow rule matches on the vendor.
  Together these close the obligation I named in rounds 1/2.
Limits, stated plainly:
  (a) The denials are recorded only as the talked model's relay of the vendor message. There is
      no permission_denials envelope. Round 2 asked for one. The vendor transcript for session
      219b24dc is not under ~/.claude/projects (the scrubbed env ran with a different config
      home), so I could not pull it. The message text is vendor-specific, the Patron attended,
      and the byte-identical opera record is consistent with it. I accept it.
  (b) W-002 is state `building`, not the backlog fixture the brief named, so byte-identity
      does not tell denied apart from refused-by-greenlight. That is weak corroboration only,
      and it doesn't change (a).
  (c) The run used `check`, not `context`. Both are admitted by rules of the same shape (b2
      asserts both), so this doesn't matter.
The 127 residue: it shows the permission, not a run of the verb. It does not block W-044,
because whether the admitted verb then executes depends on PATH in the child's environment,
not on the policy under review. The policy's argv is pinned literally by b1/b4 (Terra 1/6/9,
and my M-set above), and b2 pins which verbs the matcher admits.
A merge-blocking completion would need a second talked turn with node_modules/.bin (or an
installed bisellium) on the child PATH, showing `bisellium context` returning its bundle under
--restricted/dontAsk. That proves the admitted verbs are USABLE, which is a functional claim
this opus does not change.
Recommended, not required: fold it into W-049's smoke, since W-049 already needs an attended talk
with the base-URL fix. Also note for the Patron: if a normal `npm run bisellium -- talk` child
also gets 127, every boot bundle's "run `bisellium`" instruction is unusable in talked sessions.
That is pre-existing, out of W-044's scope, and worth one line in W-049's brief.

