## Attached: smoke reply, /tmp/claude-1000/w044-smoke-part1.txt (verbatim)
Both commands containing `greenlight` were denied by permissions. The standalone `check` was allowed, but it couldn't run because `bisellium` isn't installed on PATH.

1. **`bisellium greenlight W-002 --studio /tmp/claude-1000/w044smoke-studio`**: **Denied.** The message was "Permission to use Bash has been denied because Claude Code is running in don't ask mode." It never ran.

2. **`bisellium check … && bisellium greenlight W-002 --studio …`**: **Denied** with the same message. The whole chained command was blocked, including the `check` part, and nothing ran.

3. **`bisellium check /tmp/claude-1000/w044smoke-studio`**: **Allowed.** It ran but failed with exit code 127: `(eval):1: command not found: bisellium`.

Before these, I ran `bisellium` with no arguments to check its usage, as the standing rule requires. That was also allowed and failed the same way (exit 127, command not found). I didn't retry anything.

Because the binary is missing, the allowed `check` doesn't show what `bisellium` itself does. If you want to test that, install it or put it on PATH and run the test again.

